# Movement-Hindrance Traps Implementation Plan

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** Add eight trap pieces that cost the opponent **time**, not health — Slick Floor, Slow Field, Darkness Zone, Trapdoor, One-Way Gate, Push Tile, Teleport-Back Pair, and Greased Walls — each with a distinct editor color and glyph so a maze author can read their maze at a glance.

**Architecture:** Every trap declares `Dir.N | Dir.S` openings, so it is *already* a straight corridor as far as `PieceDB`, `MazeValidator`, `DungeonBuilder.AddPiece` and the editor's placement rules are concerned — **none of that code needs to change**. A trap is corridor geometry plus dressing plus one `Area3D`. `TrapZone` (an `Area3D`) registers itself with `PlayerController` on entry and deregisters on exit; the player recomputes a small struct of movement modifiers from whichever zones it is currently standing in. Traps that are not modifier-shaped — Trapdoor, One-Way Gate, Teleport — carry their own tiny behaviour script on the same node.

**Tech Stack:** Godot 4.6.2 Mono (C#). Trap dressing is procedural `SurfaceTool` geometry — no imported models, and no animated GLBs (the engine setup here does not support them; see `CLAUDE.md` Limitations).

